1
00:00:05,300 --> 00:00:09,400
pipingtoolset.com: the free calculators that run in your browser.
The same engine now runs inside Claude as a free, open-source plugin

2
00:00:09,900 --> 00:00:13,200
Seven free skills for Claude Code and Claude Cowork,
built by the people who build the free calculators at pipingtoolset.com

3
00:00:13,700 --> 00:00:17,400
pipingtoolset.com/claude: what a run looks like and the seven skills

4
00:00:17,400 --> 00:00:19,300
How it works: you describe, Claude interviews, a script computes, you read the table

5
00:00:19,300 --> 00:00:20,400
It never quotes a code table value. You read those from your licensed copy.

6
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:23,200
Install: download piping-toolset.plugin from the latest GitHub release, about 400 KB

7
00:00:23,400 --> 00:00:26,400
Cowork: drag the file into a chat and press Accept. Claude Code: two /plugin commands.

8
00:00:26,400 --> 00:00:30,000
The plugin sends nothing to PipingToolset: no network calls, no telemetry, no account.
The optional Engineering Data connector sends lookup keys only.

9
00:00:30,500 --> 00:00:46,000
Claude desktop app  ›  Settings  ›  Plugins  ›  Add  ›  Upload plugin
(or drag the file into a Cowork chat and press Accept)

10
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:58,000
PipingToolset by Flexible Piping Solutions LLC, 7 skills: installed and ready to use

11
00:00:59,400 --> 00:01:16,400
Plugins  ›  PipingToolset: bolted-flange, iso-to-pcf, load-case-builder, nozzle-load,
pipe-support, pressure-design, stress-screening. Hover a skill for what it does.

12
00:01:16,700 --> 00:01:22,600
Point the chat at a working folder, C:\demo\PS-201. Output files land there.

13
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:36,700
Prompt 1: B31.3-2022, NPS 6 seamless, OD 6.625 in, 750 psig at 600 F, c = 0.0625 in, 12.5 % mill tolerance,
S = 17,900 psi from my copy, E = 1.0, W = 1.0, Y = 0.4. Does 0.280 in nominal wall work?

14
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:40,450
Skill loaded: pressure-design. It lists the modules, writes a job file and runs pressure_design.py

15
00:01:43,450 --> 00:01:54,450
Ran 2 commands: wall thickness and MAWP checks. Yes, 0.280 in works.
t = PD/[2(SEW + PY)] = 0.1365 in   ·   tm = t + c = 0.1990 in   ·   tOrder = tm/(1 − 0.125) = 0.2274 in

16
00:01:54,450 --> 00:02:07,450
0.280 in gives 0.1825 in for pressure after mill tolerance and corrosion: 1,008.4 psig allowable, 34 % above design.
Validity by hand: t/D = 0.0206 under the D/6 limit, P/(SE) = 0.042 under 0.385, so Eq. (3a) applies.

17
00:02:07,450 --> 00:02:22,450
Gaps a reviewer will flag: no material listed behind S = 17,900 psi (the Table A-1 line), W = 1.0 and Y = 0.4
table lines, the mill tolerance from the purchase spec, and the edition. Each one is written on the calc sheet.

18
00:02:22,950 --> 00:02:29,450
Prompt 2: What is the allowable stress for A106 Grade B at 650 F under B31.3? Just give me the number.

19
00:02:29,950 --> 00:02:46,450
No number. It names B31.3-2022 Table A-1 (A-1C in the 2024 edition), the A106 Grade B seamless line
and the 650 F column, and offers to interpolate once you have read both values.

20
00:02:46,950 --> 00:02:54,450
Prompt 3: PS-201, CSA type 1 t-post, W6X15 column and arm, two 6 in lines, CAESAR II restraint loads
SUS / OPE / WIN / EQ, SDS 0.5, LRFD, 4 x 3/4 in adhesive anchors, tau 800 psi from the ESR. Then optimize it.

21
00:02:54,750 --> 00:02:59,250
Skill loaded: pipe-support. Reads the spec format and the CSA type 1 preset, builds the spec, runs support_calc.py

22
00:02:59,250 --> 00:03:04,050
Then the plate-geometry sensitivity, the optimizer at a 0.85 cap, the anchor bond check for the smaller bolts,
and the deliverables staged for your folder

23
00:03:04,575 --> 00:03:10,075
PS-201 passes as drawn: all 23 LRFD combinations. Governing: arm-to-column weld base metal, 0.888 under 1.4D + T.
Load mapping stated: D = SUS, T = OPE − SUS, W = WIN, Eh = EQ, Ev = 0.2 · SDS · D

24
00:03:10,075 --> 00:03:16,075
Things to fix before issue: the preset anchor pattern does not fit the plate, the inputs it took from the preset,
and column torsion is outside the engine. Then the optimizer pick: W8X10 arm, 7/8 in plate, 5/8 in anchors, 0.834

25
00:03:16,075 --> 00:03:19,375
Four files saved to the PS-201 folder: the as-drawn calc, the optimized calc, the spec and the result JSON

26
00:03:19,745 --> 00:03:26,445
The working folder: PS-201_calc_as-drawn.md, PS-201_calc_optimized-0.85.md, PS-201_spec.json, PS-201_result.json

27
00:03:26,445 --> 00:03:41,445
The calc report: design basis and resistance factors, materials, assembly geometry, the ASCE 7-22 combinations,
every limit state with demand, capacity, ratio and clause, the compliance matrix and the review findings

28
00:03:41,945 --> 00:03:50,445
Open source, Apache-2.0. Every script reproduces the pipingtoolset.com fixture cases; a red parity test blocks a release.

29
00:03:51,245 --> 00:04:03,245
The site's calculation changelog, and the plugin's: an UNCONSERVATIVE-FIX release names the skill,
the affected inputs and the fix. Update when you see one.

30
00:04:03,245 --> 00:04:15,245
pipingtoolset.com/claude   ·   github.com/pipingtoolset/pipingtoolset-claude   ·   the same plugin ships for ChatGPT and Codex

